    "text": "To solve the expression \\(\\frac{1}{-6} \\cdot 6 \\cdot 7 + 8 \\cdot 2 \\div 8 \\cdot (7-1)\\), we will follow the order of operations (PEMDAS/BODMAS: Parentheses/Brackets, Exponents/Orders, Multiplication and Division (from left to right), Addition and Subtraction (from left to right)).\n\nFirst, let's break down the expression into manageable parts and solve each part step by step.\n\n1. Calculate \\(\\frac{1}{-6} \\cdot 6\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{-6} \\cdot 6 = \\frac{1 \\cdot 6}{-6} = \\frac{6}{-6} = -1\n   \\]\n\n2. Multiply the result by 7:\n   \\[\n   -1 \\cdot 7 = -7\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate the expression inside the parentheses:\n   \\[\n   7 - 1 = 6\n   \\]\n\n4. Calculate \\(8 \\cdot 2 \\div 8 \\cdot 6\\):\n   - First, perform the multiplication and division from left to right:\n     \\[\n     8 \\cdot 2 = 16\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     16 \\div 8 = 2\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     2 \\cdot 6 = 12\n     \\]\n\n5. Now, add the results from steps 2 and 4:\n   \\[\n   -7 + 12 = 5\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
